        "end": {
          "line": 16,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The router's job",
      "description": "**What it is:** the first node. It decides *which ad network* and *what kind of task* your question is about.\n\n**How it works:** a fast, cheap model call classifies the question into a route like `admob_reporting` or `admanager_orders`.\n\n**Why it matters:** everything after this depends on the route: which tools load, which instructions the specialist gets, and which model runs.\n\n**Watch out:** nine networks are listed, but how deep each one goes depends on its tools (tour 10)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 55,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 31,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 55,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "title": "Networks and what each can do",
      "description": "**How it works:** `NETWORK_CAPABILITIES` lists what you can ask about for each network: AdMob has inventory, reporting, mediation and experimentation; Ad Manager has inventory, reporting, orders, deals and targeting; the others mostly have inventory and reporting.\n\n**Why it matters:** this table defines every possible route, and later it filters which tools the specialist sees.\n\n**Watch out:** this list lives in Python. The frontend has its own network list (`supported-networks.json`). Keep them in step."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 86,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 62,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 86,
          "character": 31
        }
      },
      "title": "Building every route name",
      "description": "**How it works:** for each network and each of its capabilities, it makes a route name like `unity_mediation` mapped to `(\"unity\", \"mediation\")`. It adds `general` (no network needed) and two cross-network routes: `orchestration_mediation` and `orchestration_reporting`.\n\n**Why it matters:** the model only has to output a route name. This map turns it into a service and capability the code can use.\n\n**Watch out:** it's built once, when the module loads. Change the table and you must restart the agent."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 176,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 147,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 176,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "title": "Preparing the question",
      "description": "**How it works:** if the run was sent back with a new query (a topic change), that query is used, though nothing triggers that yet (tour 07). Then it gathers recent context and your connected networks, creates the fast model (`haiku` role, temperature 0, max 150 tokens), and builds two messages: the router instructions and your question with context.\n\n**Why it matters:** temperature 0 makes routing consistent. The same question gets the same route.\n\n**Watch out:** `get_router_prompt()` is called **outside** the `try` below. If the prompt can't be fetched (next step), the whole run fails instead of falling back."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/utils/prompts.py",
      "line": 118,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 110,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 118,
          "character": 18
        }
      },
      "title": "The prompts live in LangSmith",
      "description": "**What it is:** where the router's instructions come from.\n\n**How it works:** `pull_prompt(\"adagent-router\")` downloads the prompt from LangSmith's prompt hub (and caches it). The specialist's role, instructions and template are pulled the same way.\n\n**Why it matters:** prompts can be changed in LangSmith without redeploying the agent.\n\n**Watch out:** there's no local fallback. No `LANGSMITH_API_KEY`, or a missing prompt, means the agent can't answer. The prompt text is pushed from `backend/scripts/push_prompts_to_langsmith.py`, so that's where to read it."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 131,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 105,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 131,
          "character": 50
        }
      },
      "title": "Reading the model's answer",
      "description": "**How it works:** the model must reply in three lines: `THINKING:` (its reasoning), `ROUTE:` (a route name) and `PATH:` (`reactive` or `workflow`). Unknown routes become `general`; unknown paths become `workflow`.\n\n**Why it matters:** plain labelled lines are easy to parse and cheap to generate. No JSON mode needed.\n\n**Watch out:** the default path is `workflow`, the safer and more capable (and more expensive) one. When in doubt, the code spends more to get it right."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 195,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 180,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 195,
          "character": 105
        }
      },
      "title": "Asking the model",
      "description": "**How it works:** send the two messages, parse the reply, and record token usage for cost tracking. The result goes into `routing` in the state.\n\n**Why it matters:** this one cheap call steers the expensive specialist call that follows.\n\n**Watch out:** `ainvoke` is the async call, so the server can handle other requests while waiting. The comment mentions avoiding blocking on Windows."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 231,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 217,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 231,
          "character": 22
        }
      },
      "title": "When routing fails",
      "description": "**How it works:** on any error, the route becomes `general` / `assistant` on the `workflow` path, with the error saved as the reasoning. The backflow flags are cleared either way.\n\n**Why it matters:** a routing failure doesn't crash the chat. You get a general answer instead of an error.\n\n**Watch out:** `general` means no ad-network tools. The user may get a polite answer that can't actually fetch their data."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/router.py",
      "line": 102,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 89,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 102,
          "character": 36
        }
      },
      "title": "How much history the router sees",
      "description": "**How it works:** only the last 6 messages, each cut to 200 characters, as plain `role: content` lines.\n\n**Why it matters:** enough to understand follow-ups like *and yesterday?*, while keeping the cheap model call cheap.\n\n**Watch out:** a long message is cut mid-sentence. The router only needs the gist."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/entity_loader.py",
      "line": 10,
