        }
      },
      "title": "The entity loader's job",
      "description": "**What it is:** the second node. It loads *your real* accounts, apps and ad units before the AI does anything.\n\n**How it works:** the docstring names the idea: *entity grounding*. Pre-load the real IDs, put them in the prompt, and validate tool inputs against them.\n\n**Why it matters:** AI models invent plausible-looking IDs. Giving it the real list is the main defence.\n\n**Watch out:** grounding helps, but the final check is the tool executor's validation (tour 11)."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/entity_loader.py",
      "line": 61,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 39,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 61,
          "character": 64
        }
      },
      "title": "One call for everything",
      "description": "**How it works:** one GET to the API's `/api/internal/entities` with the shared secret, returning your AdMob accounts (with apps) and Ad Manager networks. The API caches the answer for 5 minutes.\n\n**Why it matters:** one cached call instead of many keeps every message fast.\n\n**Watch out:** on any error it returns empty lists, so the user sees *no accounts connected* instead of an error message."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/entity_loader.py",
      "line": 229,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 193,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 229,
          "character": 19
        }
      },
      "title": "Flattening the data",
      "description": "**How it works:** each AdMob account becomes one entry in `accounts`, its apps go into `apps`, and its ad units go into `ad_units`, each tagged with its account. Ad Manager networks become accounts too.\n\n**Why it matters:** flat lists are easy to search and to check tool inputs against.\n\n**Watch out:** ad units are **not** in the cached call. They're fetched with one extra request per AdMob account, one after another. With many accounts, that adds up."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/entity_loader.py",
      "line": 296,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 267,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 296,
          "character": 10
        }
      },
      "title": "Stopping when you're not connected",
      "description": "**How it works:** if the router chose a network you haven't connected, it creates a **blocking** `action_required` with a link to `/providers`. If you have no accounts at all, the same happens, suggesting AdMob.\n\n**Why it matters:** the graph stops here (tour 07), so no tokens are spent, and the UI shows a *Connect* button instead of a vague answer.\n\n**Watch out:** `general` and `orchestration` routes skip this check."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/nodes/entity_loader.py",
      "line": 416,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 408,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 416,
          "character": 4
        }
      },
      "title": "The prompt section it writes",
      "description": "**How it works:** `build_entity_system_prompt` writes an *Available Entities* section: your networks, each account, up to 10 apps per account, and up to 5 ad units per app. Then the rules: in **strict** mode use only enabled accounts; in **soft** mode prefer them. It ends with *never invent or guess IDs*.\n\n**Why it matters:** this text is pasted into the specialist's system prompt (tour 09). It's what the model sees as your world.\n\n**Watch out:** the 10-app and 5-ad-unit caps keep the prompt short. If you have more, the model only sees *… and N more* and must use a list tool to find the rest."
    },
    {
      "file": "backend/chat/graph/validators.py",
      "line": 380,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 365,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 380,
          "character": 36
        }
      },
      "title": "Soft mode only warns",
      "description": "**What it is:** the general ID check every tool call goes through (`validate_entity_references`).\n\n**How it works:** IDs found in the tool's arguments are checked against your loaded entities. In **strict** mode, a bad ID blocks the call with a list of valid options. In **soft** mode, the default, it only logs a warning and lets the call through.\n\n**Why it matters:** soft mode is friendlier when the loaded list is incomplete, for example with more than 10 apps.\n\n**Watch out:** in soft mode, a made-up ID reaches the network's API, which then rejects it. Mediation groups are the exception: they get a strict pre-check of their own (tour 11)."
    },
    {
      "title": "What you can do now",
      "description": "### What you can do now\n\n- Explain how a question becomes a route, and why the router uses a cheap model at temperature 0.\n- Say where the prompts live and what happens if LangSmith is unreachable.\n- Explain entity grounding, and its limits (caps, soft mode).\n- Explain why the graph can stop before the expensive specialist call.\n\n**Next:** *09 - The specialist*. For every function in these files, see *A01 - Atlas: agent graph*."
    }
  ]
}